How this packaging relates to the upstream source
=================================================

This branch adds debian/ to the upstream repository and changes nothing else.
debian/ is the only difference against upstream main.

There is no upstream release tarball to package: the repository is a driver
tree plus two *integration* patches, and nothing in it is installable on its
own. Two other projects have to be compiled together with it:

    libfprint v1.94.10   patches/libfprint/1.94.10.patch
    fprintd   v1.94.5    patches/fprintd/1.94.5-goodix-spi.patch

Both are fetched at build time by the project's own scripts
(scripts/fetch-libfprint.sh and scripts/fetch-fprintd.sh) into
debian/build/sources, then prepared with scripts/libfprint/prepare-tree.sh and
scripts/apply-fprintd-patch.sh, which debian/rules calls unchanged. The same
scripts are what ./install.sh uses, so the tree that gets compiled here is the
tree the upstream installer compiles.

Consequences:

* The build needs network access. debian/README.Debian, section 3, states the
  reason and the pins.

* debian/source/format is "3.0 (quilt)", but a source package built with
  "dpkg-buildpackage -S" would still fail for lack of an orig tarball: the two
  upstream trees are not part of this source. Only binary builds
  ("dpkg-buildpackage -b -uc -us") are supported from this branch.

* debian/rules clean removes debian/build, and debian/source/options lists the
  same path as tar-ignore so it cannot leak into a generated source package.

* The pins are fixed (v1.94.10, v1.94.5). scripts/fetch-*.sh record the ref and
  the integration revision in the checkout's git config and refuse to reuse a
  tree that does not match, so a warm cache cannot silently change the build.

* To build offline, pre-populate debian/build/sources/libfprint and
  debian/build/sources/fprintd with git trees that carry the matching
  goodix-spi.sourceRef (and, for libfprint, goodix-spi.integrationRevision)
  configuration values; the fetch scripts then reuse them instead of cloning.

Files that deliberately are not here:

* debian/watch - upstream has no tagged release; the package version follows
  the 0.0~git<date>.<commit> snapshot scheme.
* A separate -dbgsym stanza - debhelper generates it.
